    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 5\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[22, 30, 10], [6, 13, 7], [11, 21, 22], [9, 16, 17], [8, 11, 5]]\n    machine_assignments = [[1, 2, 0], [2, 1, 0], [0, 2, 1], [1, 2, 0], [0, 2, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Job Shop Production Scheduling Challenge\n\nContext\nOur facility operates a job shop with multiple specialized workstations that support a mix of products and custom orders. For the current planning window, we need to coordinate the flow of several jobs through these workstations. Each job follows a defined routing: a series of operations that must be completed in a fixed sequence across specific machines. The duration of each operation is known in advance, and machine eligibility is predetermined.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nThe core challenge is to build a feasible, efficient schedule that sequences all operations on each machine while respecting job routings and finite machine capacity. Because machines are shared resources and several jobs compete for the same workstations, early choices about the order of operations on one machine can create bottlenecks downstream. Additionally, operation durations vary, which increases the risk of idle time on some machines and queues on others if the schedule is not carefully coordinated.\n\nOperating Rules and Constraints\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time; no overlaps or resource sharing.\n- Fixed job order: Operations within the same job must be completed in their defined sequence; a downstream operation cannot start until the previous one in the same job has finished.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts on a machine, it runs to completion without interruption or splitting.\n- Assigned resources and known durations: Every operation is tied to a specific machine and has a known processing time (see Annex for the structured data).\n- Feasibility across the shop: The schedule must simultaneously satisfy all of the above for all jobs and machines.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a synchronized production schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time from the first operation start to the final operation finish (overall completion time, or makespan). Within this goal, we will seek to reduce idle time on critical workstations and avoid creating avoidable queues.\n\nNext Steps\n- Confirm the routing and timing data for each job and operation.\n- Build a machine-by-machine sequence that respects job order, machine exclusivity, and non-interruption.\n- Validate the schedule for feasibility and iterate to reduce the overall completion time.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 5\n- Number of machines (workstations): 3\n- Machine assignments by job and operation: Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 2\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 1]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 1]\n- Processing times by job and operation: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 3 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 22, Op2: 30, Op3: 10]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 6, Op2: 13, Op3: 7]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 11, Op2: 21, Op3: 22]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 9, Op2: 16, Op3: 17]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: 8, Op2: 11, Op3: 5]",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Job Shop Production Scheduling Challenge\n\nContext\nOur facility operates a job shop with multiple specialized workstations that support a mix of products and custom orders. For the current planning window, we need to coordinate the flow of several jobs through these workstations. Each job follows a defined routing: a series of operations that must be completed in a fixed sequence across specific machines. The duration of each operation is known in advance, and machine eligibility is predetermined.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nThe core challenge is to build a feasible, efficient schedule that sequences all operations on each machine while respecting job routings and finite machine capacity. Because machines are shared resources and several jobs compete for the same workstations, early choices about the order of operations on one machine can create bottlenecks downstream. Additionally, operation durations vary, which increases the risk of idle time on some machines and queues on others if the schedule is not carefully coordinated.\n\nOperating Rules and Constraints\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time; no overlaps or resource sharing.\n- Fixed job order: Operations within the same job must be completed in their defined sequence; a downstream operation cannot start until the previous one in the same job has finished.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts on a machine, it runs to completion without interruption or splitting.\n- Assigned resources and known durations: Every operation is tied to a specific machine and has a known processing time (see Annex for the structured data).\n- Feasibility across the shop: The schedule must simultaneously satisfy all of the above for all jobs and machines.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a synchronized production schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time from the first operation start to the final operation finish (overall completion time, or makespan). Within this goal, we will seek to reduce idle time on critical workstations and avoid creating avoidable queues.\n\nNext Steps\n- Confirm the routing and timing data for each job and operation.\n- Build a machine-by-machine sequence that respects job order, machine exclusivity, and non-interruption.\n- Validate the schedule for feasibility and iterate to reduce the overall completion time.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Machine assignments by job and operation: {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Processing times by job and operation: {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
